SCOPE
-----

The MIT License below governs this repository in full.

Three things are not in this repository at all, because they are built from a
separate, private crate called `talus-enterprise`:

  * the automatic response path — terminating or suspending a detected process
  * the web dashboard, its REST API, its WebSocket stream and Prometheus metrics
  * the Kafka, ClickHouse and MemGraph exporters, and the C FFI library

This is a boundary of distribution, not of configuration. There is no flag to
pass and no check to remove: a build of this crate contains no `kill(2)` call,
no dashboard and no exporter, because that source is not here. The seam exists
in two traits — `monitor::Responder` and `storage::EventSink` — whose default
implementations observe without acting, so a MIT build reaches a detection
verdict and reports it as unacted.

Everything that makes the detection trustworthy is here and stays MIT: the eBPF
probes, the scoring and sweep detectors, the MeMLP engine, the signed audit
chain, the self-sandbox, the watchdog, the TUI, the JSON and plain-text output,
the storage pipeline, the CLI, and the FFI needed to read the audit chain.

Nothing in this repository's history changes retroactively. MIT is a grant; see
`NOTICE`.

THE MIT LICENSE.

That means, specifically:

  * the `kill_process` function and the response-enforcement logic around it in
    `process-monitor/src/monitor.rs`, and
  * the code in `process-monitor/src/ffi.rs` that gates that path on a
    commercial entitlement,

are licensed under the separate terms in `LICENSE.commercial`, not under MIT.

Everything else — the eBPF probes, the scoring and sweep detectors, the audit
chain, the sandbox, the web dashboard, the REST API, the WebSocket stream, the
SIEM exporters, the CLI, the TUI and the FFI library used to embed Talus — is
MIT, permanently and without further condition. That is the whole detection
engine, and it stays open source whatever happens to the commercial tier.

Nothing in this exception changes the licence of any file in the repository's
history before this notice was added. MIT is a grant; see `NOTICE` for what that
means for copies obtained earlier.

